FADE IN:

# ACT ONE

= Ordinary world. We meet the people. Something happens that they cannot ignore. The act ends when they are locked into the story (~page 25 of a 110-page feature; earlier in a short).

## Set Up

= Who are they? What do they already want? What must they not lose?

[[ SET UP
Not a scene yet — the job of the first pages.
Show the world as it is, then crack it.
Want (what they chase) is not the same as need (what they must learn). ]]

## Sequence 1

= A sequence is a run of scenes with one job. Scenes are the steps. Sequences live inside acts. Acts live inside the story.

[[ SCENE HEADINGS
General → specific, left to right.
INT. or EXT. · CITY · BUILDING · ROOM · TIME
INT. = inside. EXT. = outside. EST. = a wide view. I/E. = both (a car, a doorway).
New heading when the place or the time changes.
Fountain prints the line in CAPITALS. ]]

INT. LONDON - WATERLOO - PLATFORM 2 - DAY

A board FLICKERS. Late commuters hug the yellow line. A paper coffee cup rolls, stops against a shoe.

MAYA (30s) does not pick it up. She watches the far tunnel. One bag. No phone out.

[[ ACTION
Show it. Do not explain it.
Present tense. What a camera can see or a mic can hear.
Three or four lines, then a breath. One beat per sentence.
If you cannot film the word "and", cut the word "and".
First time we meet someone, their NAME is in CAPITALS. ]]

A WHISTLE. The crowd tightens.

GUARD (O.S.)
Mind the gap.

MAYA
(to the empty track)
Don't you start.

[[ SPEECH
Character cue: NAME alone, capitals, after a blank line.
(O.S.) they are in the scene, not in shot. (V.O.) we hear them, may not see them speak.
Parenthetical: how the line is said, or a tiny bit of business. One phrase.
Dialogue: no quotation marks. If they would not say it, cut it.
Same spelling of the name every time. ]]

The train SHOVES in. Doors open. Maya does not move.

JON (40s, cheap suit, wrong platform energy) stops beside her.

JON
You getting on, or posing?

MAYA
You first.

They speak over each other as the alarm BEEPS.

MAYA
I said—

JON ^
After you.

[[ Dual dialogue: put ^ after the *second* character name. Preview prints the two speeches side by side. ]]

She steps on. He does not. The doors close on her look.

>CUT TO:

## Sequence 2

= Pressure arrives. The first problem that will not wait.

[[ Write the next scene heading here.
A new INT./EXT. line is usually enough — you do not need CUT TO: every time.
Use a transition when the *way* you leave the scene matters. ]]

## Sequence 3: The Cozzers

= The law, or the thing that acts like the law, enters the frame. No going back to the old life.

# ACT TWO

= They pursue a plan. It works, then it costs. The middle is not "more stuff" — it is rising pressure with a turn in the centre.

## Sequence 4: The Alibi

= The story they will tell if this goes wrong. Plant it before you need it.

## Sequence 5: The Plan

= The job as they understand it. Roles. Timing. What they think success looks like.

# Midpoint

= The story flips. A false victory or a hard truth. After this, the old plan is dead even if they have not admitted it yet. Around the middle of the page count.

## Sequence 6: The Robbery

= The plan meets the world. Something they did not budget for walks in.

# ACT THREE

= Payoff. No new machinery unless you planted it. They choose, they pay, the world is different.

## Sequence 7: The Escape

= Getting out is harder than getting in. The clock is still running.

## Sequence 8: The Set Up

= Who was playing whom. The bill comes due.

## Sequence 9

= Aftermath. The personal cost, not another set-piece.

## Sequence 10

= Last image. Echo the first image if you can. Then stop.

>FADE OUT.

>THE END<
